[
  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-99056",
      "knowledge_point_id": "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions",
      "problem": "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+6x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "$x=1$ and $x=2$",
