  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-132567",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
