  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-41263",
      "knowledge_point_id": "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/34b473c8e212297d8cb434fe0a2dd9872cfa2fda924ac3a4d528bc1318af8c5f.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=8$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},4).$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
