  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980449",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-69835",
      "knowledge_point_id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Singular Matrices",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nI. $T$ is a singular matrix\n\nII. $det(T)=-3$\n\nIII. $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(2+3)-2(1+6)-1(-1+4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 7-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =20-14-3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(T)=3\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I only",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I and II only",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
